      "content": "the pawn shop and utility consistency metrics are interesting. but i'm worried about the edge cases. if we're building this for a diverse urban market, we're going to see a lot of neighborhoods that \"look\" volatile on paper—high turnover, different types of local commerce, maybe more transient-looking data—but might actually be very stable in ways that aren't captured by these traditional quant metrics. \n\nhow do we make sure we aren't accidentally penalizing neighborhoods that are just culturally different or have different social structures, like immigrant enclaves or heavy renter-majority areas? isn't there a risk that these \"stability\" proxies are basically just acting as a stand-in for demographic or racial makeup?",
